{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "A 4 Hz square-wave clock (function generator, 0-5 V) drives a 4-bit binary counter (output resistance lowered to 5 ohm so each output can source a whole row). Q0..Q3 drive the anode rows R1, R3, R5 and R7 of an 8x8 row-anode LED matrix; every column cathode returns to ground through its own 330 ohm resistor (~9 mA per LED). Each counter bit therefore lights a full horizontal bar: Q0 (top bar) toggles every clock, Q3 (bottom bar) every 8 clocks, so the matrix shows the count in binary, least significant bit at the top. The even rows are left unconnected.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "LED Matrix Binary Bar Graph"
  },
  "optics": {
